<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/intermediate/oceans-and-currents">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The ocean is <strong>the planet's heat and carbon flywheel</strong>: wind-driven surface
    gyres redistribute heat in years; the density-driven deep circulation does it in centuries.
    It covers 71% of the surface, holds 97% of the water, contains 50–80% of all life, and has
    absorbed about 90% of the excess heat and a quarter of the CO₂ of the industrial era. The
    atmosphere is the ocean's loud, fast, thin companion — weather is hours, ocean circulation
    is years to millennia — and everything climate-related runs on ocean physics. This lesson
    covers that physics from the shore inward: waves, tides, the currents great and small, and
    the coastlines they build and unbuild.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="heat budget, gyres, the conveyor, ENSO" widget="equationStep" config={oceanCfg} />

  <h2>Waves: energy in transit, water standing still</h2>
  <p>
    Ocean waves are made by wind — duration, fetch (the distance of open water the wind blows
    over) and wind speed set their size — but the water in a wave barely travels. Each parcel
    runs a small circular orbit, rising and falling as the wave passes, passing the energy
    along: a wave is a travelling shape, not a travelling cargo (a stadium "wave" is the same
    physics with people). The orbital motion dies with depth — at depths beyond about half the
    wavelength the water is still, which is why submarines glide beneath storms. Watch the
    profile change with amplitude:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="a wave is a moving shape — the surface oscillates while the water beneath orbits in place" widget="plotter" config={waveCfg} />

  <p>
    Everything changes where depth runs out. When a wave reaches water shallower than about
    half its wavelength, the orbit drags on the bottom: the wave slows, its wavelength
    compresses, its energy concentrates, and the crest steepens until it topples — a
    <strong>breaker</strong>. This is why waves always arrive roughly parallel to the beach
    (refraction bends them toward the shallows) and why surf exists at all. The same
    bottom-drag makes waves approach headlands from all directions and concentrate their
    energy there — the first lesson in why headlands erode.
  </p>

  <h2>The ocean in layers: why the deep is a different world</h2>
  <p>
    The ocean is not one body of water but stacked ones. The sun-warmed <strong>surface
    layer</strong> (top ~100–200 m) is mixed by wind and waves, holds the oxygen exchange and
    the phytoplankton. Beneath it a sharp density jump — the <strong>pycnocline</strong> —
    separates it from the vast, cold, nearly unchanging <strong>deep ocean</strong> (2–4 °C
    almost everywhere, from pole to pole, because cold water is dense water and the deep is
    filled by the polar sinking of the conveyor). Temperature, not latitude, governs the
    deep: a diver off the Maldives descends into water as cold as the North Atlantic's.
    Salinity sets fine differences (evaporation concentrates the subtropics' salt; rivers and
    rain dilute the tropics; the Mediterranean, a hot evaporation basin, runs saltier than the
    Atlantic and spills its dense brine over the Gibraltar sill). The layering is why the
    ocean's carbon and heat storage work at all: the deep is a warehouse almost sealed from
    the surface except where the conveyor's upwelling opens it — and why nutrients sent down
    in sinking organisms return to the light only at upwelling zones, which is exactly where
    the fisheries are.
  </p>

  <h2>Tides and currents: the shore's daily machinery</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Tides</strong> — the Moon-and-eclipses lesson traced the geometry: the Moon's
    differential pull raises two ocean bulges, the rotating Earth passes through them, most
    coasts see two high tides a day, and the Sun's contribution makes spring and neap tides on
    a 14.8-day beat. Geography modulates the standard curve: the Bay of Fundy's funnel shape
    concentrates its tide to a 16-metre range — the world's largest — while enclosed seas like
    the Mediterranean barely move. Tidal currents in narrow channels can run faster than a
    kayaker paddles; mills have ground grain on them since the 12th century, and tidal power
    stations (La Rance, France; Sihwa, South Korea) still harvest the Moon's pull.
    <strong>Currents</strong> proper are the ocean's circulation, in two layers. Wind-driven
    <strong>surface currents</strong> spin the five great subtropical <strong>gyres</strong> —
    clockwise in the Northern Hemisphere, anticlockwise in the Southern, steered by the trade
    winds and westerlies and bent by Coriolis. The gyres' western edges run fast, narrow and
    deep: the <strong>Gulf Stream</strong> (up to 2.5 m/s — you can swim slower than the
    Atlantic off Florida), the Kuroshio off Japan, the Agulhas off South Africa. The Gulf
    Stream carries about 100 times the flow of the Amazon and delivers northern Europe
    several degrees of maritime warmth — the reason palm trees survive in Ireland at the
    latitude of Labrador. On the eastern side, <strong>upwelling</strong> lifts cold,
    nutrient-rich deep water to the surface (off Peru, California, Namibia), feeding the
    world's richest fisheries — a fact El Niño overturns every few years.
  </p>

  <h2>The deep circulation: the global conveyor</h2>
  <p>
    Below the wind-blown layer runs circulation driven by <strong>density</strong> —
    <em>thermohaline</em> (temperature and salt) circulation. In the North Atlantic near
    Greenland and around Antarctica, surface water cools in winter; cold water is denser, and
    salt excluded from forming sea ice makes the brine below denser still. This cold, salty
    water sinks, spills southward along the seafloor, and spreads through the deep basins of
    all the oceans, rising slowly to the surface elsewhere — the <strong>global conveyor
    belt</strong> (Wallace Broecker's term, 1991). The full round trip takes roughly 1,000
    years, and it is the planet's slow organ of heat and carbon distribution: a water molecule
    in the deep ocean may not see daylight again for a millennium. The conveyor also explains
    ocean chemistry's uniformity — the salts, oxygen and nutrients of the deep sea are set by
    what sinking water carried down, centuries ago. The Atlantic branch (the AMOC) is the most
    watched subsystem: it warms Europe, and freshwater from melting ice dilutes the salty
    sinking water that drives it. A full collapse this century is assessed as unlikely but not
    impossible (IPCC) — the tipping point most cited in climate policy.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the Gulf Stream is a river of warm water that keeps Europe
    warm, and it will simply 'switch off'." The European warmth is real but shared between the
    current, the prevailing westerlies that deliver it, and heat released by the ocean over
    the whole basin; and the conveyor is not a machine with a switch but a density-driven
    circulation that can weaken, shift and reorganise — which past climate records show it
    has done, abruptly, during deglaciations.</p>

  <h2>Coastlines: the contested boundary</h2>
  <p>
    A coast is where the ocean spends its energy, and every landform is a memo from that
    contest. <strong>Erosional coasts</strong> (rocky, high-energy) are carved by the waves'
    hydraulic hammering and abrasion: cliffs retreat, wave-cut platforms flatten at their
    feet, and headlands — which concentrate wave energy, as refraction showed — cave into
    <strong>sea caves, arches, stacks and stumps</strong> in sequence as erosion attacks the
    weakest line. <strong>Depositional coasts</strong> (low-energy) are built where currents
    drop their load: <strong>beaches</strong> are the transient storage (sand in summer,
    stripped by winter storms and replaced next year); <strong>spits</strong> grow where the
    shore drifts past a bay mouth; <strong>barrier islands</strong> — long sand bars a few
    kilometres offshore — front much of the world's Atlantic and Gulf coasts, absorbing the
    ocean's first punch; <strong>tombolos</strong> tie islands to the mainland. The engine of
    most of this is <strong>longshore drift</strong>: waves arriving at an angle push swash up
    the beach diagonally and drag backwash straight down, so sand zigzags along the shore in a
    current parallel to the coast — metres per day in stormy seasons. One consequence explains
    whole economies: a coast supplied with drift-sand accumulates beaches and tourist seasons;
    a coast downdrift of every groyne field loses both — which is why coastal management is
    always regional, and why "whose sand is this?" is a genuine legal question in several
    countries.
  </p>

  <h2>Human interference with shoreline processes</h2>
  <p>
    Because coasts move and property does not, humans intervene — and the interventions
    reliably redistribute the problem. A <strong>groyne</strong> (a wall perpendicular to the
    shore) traps longshore drift on its updrift side: the beach widens there — and the downdrift
    beach, starved of sand, erodes faster, so the next town builds a groyne, and the problem
    marches down the coast. <strong>Seawalls</strong> reflect wave energy, protecting the land
    behind while scouring the beach in front until they undermine themselves.
    <strong>Breakwaters</strong> and artificial <strong>beach nourishment</strong> (pumping
    sand — Miami Beach is an artificial beach, renourished on a cycle) work better but are
    perpetual expenses. Meanwhile <strong>dams trap the sediment</strong> rivers once delivered
    (the Nile's delta is shrinking since the Aswan High Dam; the Mississippi's wetlands,
    starved, sink), and rising seas raise the stakes. The engineering lesson generalises:
    sediment systems are connected end to end, and a wall is always a subsidy taken from
    somewhere downstream.
  </p>

  <h2>ENSO: the oscillation that steers the world</h2>
  <p>
    The Pacific runs a coupled ocean–atmosphere heartbeat. In neutral years the trade winds
    blow westward, piling warm water in the western Pacific and letting cold, nutrient-rich
    water upwell off Peru. Every 2–7 years the trades weaken (El Niño): the warm pool sloshes
    east, Peruvian upwelling fails (the name comes from the Christmas-time warm current that
    killed the anchovy fishery), and the rearranged convection rewrites weather worldwide —
    drought in Australia and Indonesia, floods in Peru, a warm winter over Canada, weaker
    monsoons. The cold phase (La Niña) roughly mirrors it. The <strong>ENSO cycle</strong> is
    the most consequential natural climate pattern on Earth — not a weather event but a
    reorganisation of the whole tropical engine, forecastable months ahead (one of the few
    genuine climate predictions) — and its global temperature fingerprint (a few tenths of a
    degree) must be subtracted from any single-year climate claim.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The ocean's quantitative role in climate bears exact numbers. Water's heat capacity is
    about four times air's per kilogram and the ocean's mass is ~250 times the atmosphere's:
    the top 3 metres of the ocean store as much heat as the <em>entire atmosphere</em>. That
    is why "the ocean absorbed 90% of global warming's excess heat" is not a consolation but
    the mechanism — it is why surface warming looks slow and why committed warming extends
    centuries into the future. The same absorption carries a chemical price: dissolved CO₂
    forms carbonic acid, and surface pH has already fallen about 0.1 units — a ~30% rise in
    acidity — with measurable thinning in corals and shellfish larvae (calcium carbonate
    dissolves more readily as pH falls). Sea level is the ocean's integrator: about 20 cm
    since 1900, now accelerating, from thermal expansion (roughly half) plus melting land ice
    — measured by satellite altimetry to millimetres, the cleanest number in climate science.
    And the map is still blank: about a quarter of the seafloor is mapped to modern resolution
    (Seabed 2030 is fixing it), while the <strong>Argo fleet</strong> — nearly 4,000 robotic
    floats drifting two kilometres down, surfacing every ten days to profile temperature and
    salinity — runs the observing system that made ocean heat content a measured quantity
    rather than an inference. The planet's largest machine is now instrumented; the next
    decade of oceanography will be the first conducted in real time.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Broecker, "The Great Ocean Conveyor" (1991); IPCC AR6 Oceans chapter; Argo programme; NOAA ENSO monitoring; OpenGeology, An Introduction to Geology, ch. 12.</p>
